<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-math" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Math</title>

  <p>
    An example of using the math element to put math inside of text is <m>\int_{1}^{2} x dx</m>.
    m is short for math.
    The way you tell pretext what math you want
    rendered in math type elements is by writing the math in latex.
    An example of using the math display element to put math on its own line is
    <md>
      \int_{1}^{2} x dx = \frac{x^{2}}{2} \Big|_{1}^{2} = \frac{1}{2}\left(4 - 1 \right) = \frac{3}{4}
    </md>.
    md is short for math display.
  </p>
  <p>
    What if we want to align multiple equations where each equation gets its own line.
    For the that we can use a math display element with additional children elements.
    Here's an example for a system of equations:
    <md>
      <mrow> 9 \amp = 4x + 2y </mrow>
      <mrow> 8 \amp = 5x + 3y </mrow>
    </md>.
    The mrow element tells pretext that its contents belong on thier own row.
    The \amp command tells pretext where the alignment point for every row is.
    In our example we aligned the equations at the equal sign.
    we can also number our equations, and or give them xml:ids.
    Here's an example:
    <md number="yes" xml:id="eq-2+3=5">
      2 + 3 = 5
    </md>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, pretext provides some useful elements for
    displaying important math results and examples.
    Here is an example of a theorem element:
  </p>
  <theorem>
    <title>Pythagorean Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In a right-angled triangle,
        the square of the hypotenuse (<m>c</m>)
        equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides.
        <md>
          a^{2} + b^{2} = c^{2}
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        This is were you would put the proof,
        but we don't have time in this tutorial.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  <p>
    Here is an example of an example element:
  </p>
  <example>
    <title>Quadratic Equation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Solve for <m>x</m>: <m>x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Try factoring the quadratic expression into two binomials.</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p><m>x = 2</m> or <m>x = 3</m></p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>Factor the expression as <m>(x - 2)(x - 3) = 0</m>. Setting each factor to zero yields <m>x = 2</m> and <m>x = 3</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <p>
    Pretext also offers several other elements
    like the two above.
    For more info check out the pretext guide.
  </p>

</section>